  "image_name": "02cf62b22e695445807da9e0c9e5010a.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways lipids act as signaling molecules in cancer-related biology. Overall, it shows that specific membrane lipids and lipid-derived mediators can control cell survival, death, growth, movement, inflammation, and tissue remodeling through both intracellular and extracellular signaling.\n\nOne part highlights cardiolipin in mitochondria. In healthy mitochondria, cardiolipin appears associated with the inner membrane and the electron transport chain, helping retain cytochrome c. After an apoptosis-inducing signal, cytochrome c is shown dispersing outward, indicating release during cell death signaling.\n\nAnother section shows acylation in WNT signaling. Lipid modification of WNT by PORCN supports signaling through Frizzled and LRP5/6 receptors. Without signaling, β-catenin is targeted for degradation; with WNT signaling, β-catenin accumulates, enters the nucleus, and drives gene expression linked here to loss of polarity and adhesion.\n\nThe lower panels emphasize extracellular lipid mediators. Lysophosphatidic acid, generated by ATX, activates LPA receptors and G proteins, promoting proliferation, motility, and survival, with PI3K–AKT signaling contributing to proliferation, angiogenesis, and metabolism. Finally, arachidonic acid–derived eicosanoids such as PGE2 signal through prostaglandin receptors to influence inflammation, immune modulation, and tissue remodeling.",
